<BaseLayout
  title="Sanying Chilli — 20,000–50,000 SHU | Tenda Peppers"
  description="Sanying (Tianying) pepper — facing-heaven chilli from Henan & Hebei at 20,000–50,000 SHU for hot sauce, chilli oil and snacks."
  jsonLd={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <div class="container section">
    <nav aria-label="Breadcrumb" style="font-size:0.9rem;color:#6b5a54;margin-bottom:16px;">
      <a href="/">Home</a> &rsaquo; <a href="/products/dried-chillies/">Dried Chillies</a> &rsaquo; Sanying
    </nav>
    <h1>Sanying (Tianying) Chilli</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The classic high-heat dry chilli — a cluster-type facing-heaven pepper at 20,000–50,000 SHU,
      grown in Henan and Hebei. The workhorse for hot sauce, chilli oil and snack seasoning.
    </p>

    <div class="takeaways">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Sanying (Tianying) is a facing-heaven cluster chilli at 20,000–50,000 SHU from Henan and Hebei — the dependable high-heat base for hot sauce, chilli oil and snack seasoning. It is the variety most buyers default to when they need high heat without the extreme burn of Jixin.
    </div>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Sanying chilli?</h2>
    <p>
      Sanying — also sold as Tianying — is a cluster-type facing-heaven chilli and the defining shape
      of the Chinese high-heat dry-chilli market. Its heat sits in the 20,000–50,000 SHU band: strong
      and lingering, but still controllable in a blend when dosed by weight. See the
      <a href="/heat-levels/high-heat/">high heat</a> page for how it fits the band.
    </p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Property</th><th>Detail</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Heat (SHU)</strong></td><td>20,000 – 50,000</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Region</strong></td><td>Henan, Hebei</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Fruit type</strong></td><td>Cluster-type facing-heaven</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Hot sauce, chilli oil, snack seasoning</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">What is Sanying pepper used for?</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Hot sauces and chilli oil</li>
      <li>Snack and instant-noodle seasoning</li>
      <li>Industrial food and processed seasoning</li>
      <li>Chilli powder and flakes (high-heat)</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Sanying vs Zidantou: sauce or powder</h2>
    <p>
      Sanying and Zidantou share the same 20,000–50,000 SHU high-heat band but suit different
      processing. Sanying is the cluster-type facing-heaven shape — the classic for hot sauce and
      chilli oil. Zidantou's conical "bullet" pod has thicker flesh that mills into a robust,
      consistent <strong>powder</strong>. If your line is sauce or oil, Sanying is the default; if it
      is heat-forward powder, Zidantou is the dependable base.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">How to specify Sanying chilli</h2>
    <p>
      Name the variety, then the form (whole, powder, flakes or segments), SHU window, moisture and
      mesh. Heat is non-linear and variety-dependent, so "Sanying, 20,000–50,000 SHU, 60–80 mesh" is a
      far more reliable order than "hot chilli powder".
    </p>
    <p class="data-note">
      Heat values are representative per variety. Confirm your exact SHU window and form when you
      inquire.
    </p>

    <h2 style="margin-top:32px;">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    <div class="faq-item">
      <h3>What is Sanying chilli?</h3>
      <p>Sanying, also called Tianying, is a cluster-type facing-heaven chilli from Henan and Hebei at 20,000–50,000 SHU. It is the classic high-heat dry-chilli shape and a workhorse for hot sauce, chilli oil and snack seasoning.</p>
    </div>
